dropped the body
--
GCC's __builtin_shuffle (a vector-extension builtin, no N5008 clause):
element i of __builtin_shuffle(v, mask) is v[mask[i] % N].  The C front
end models it (the same program as C verifies); the C++ front end reported
"symbol '__builtin_shuffle' is unknown"; it now type-checks the arguments
and reuses the C front end's implementation (so does
__builtin_shufflevector).  cpp11_dropped_library_body_warning
used exactly this call as its "unsupported construct" in a system header
and now uses a different one.
Reference compilers: g++ 13 accepts it (clang has __builtin_shufflevector
instead and rejects it).  Found by the round-163c audit of descs that
expect a rejection or a drop.  Fails on the round-162 snapshot bin51.
